# Compliance with OCP Tenets

## Openness

This profile uses publicly specified evidence formats and enables
interoperable verification across platform and device vendors.

## Efficiency

This profile combines platform and device evidence into one artifact while
preserving native device evidence, reducing repeated external collection and
format-conversion operations.

## Impact

This profile provides a common method for conveying platform inventory
attestation evidence from heterogeneous devices.

## Scale

This profile supports flat and recursive platform composition and uses detached
evidence to accommodate platforms containing multiple independently attesting
devices.

## Sustainability

This profile reuses existing EAT, CWT, COSE, CMW, and native device evidence
formats, enabling incremental adoption without replacing existing device
attestation implementations.

# Introduction

## Problem Statement

Platform attestation today is commonly disaggregated. Where per-device Evidence
is retrieved independently, attesting a whole platform requires separate
transactions that return independently protected responses. The Verifier must
then reconstruct the relationship between those components, the encompassing
platform, and the freshness challenge. There is no single signed,
platform-scoped artifact that binds a Verifier nonce, the Lead Attester state,
and the reported Sub-Attester Evidence set.

Four gaps follow:

1. No aggregate. The Verifier reassembles the composite system from independent
   evidence artifacts.
2. No uniform carriage. Sub-Attesters may expose SPDM signed measurements, emit
   their own signed EAT, or themselves produce a Composite EAT. A common
   structure is needed without rewriting the protected native evidence.
3. Lead Attester overhead. Requiring the Lead Attester to implement every
   Sub-Attester protocol solely to describe itself increases trusted-computing
   base size and complexity.
4. External orchestration. Retrieving and correlating Evidence independently
   from every component requires repeated externally visible operations and
   exposes internal topology and transport details to the Verifier.

## Approach

A Lead Attester, following the composite-device pattern in Section 3.3 of
[@{ietf-rfc9334}], produces one signed Composite EAT. The token carries the Lead
Attester's measurements and, for every Sub-Attester, a detached-submodule digest
that commits to its evidence. Native evidence is preserved and conveyed in CMW
Records under CWT claim 299 in detached Claims-Sets within a Detached EAT Bundle
(CBOR tag 602, [@{ietf-rfc9711}]).

The signed digests bind the detached Claims-Sets. Native signatures preserve
the provenance of each Sub-Attester Evidence item. Evidence collection and
bundle assembly MAY be performed outside the Lead Attester without granting
those functions authority to produce or appraise native Evidence.

The resulting bundle provides one platform-scoped artifact for a collection
event. The signed main token binds the Lead Attester identity, Lead Attester
measurements, freshness challenge, reported Target Environment Identifiers, and
digests of the included detached Claims-Sets. This reduces external collection
transactions and topology-dependent orchestration while preserving native
Evidence provenance. It does not replace native Evidence validation or
appraisal against independently trusted expected-composition information.

## Scope

This document specifies the Composite EAT evidence format for platform inventory
attestation: the signed main token, detached native-Evidence carriage, digest
binding, processing rules, and supported Evidence forms. It supports flat and
recursive platform composition.

Appraisal against composition endorsements and reference values is described
informatively and specified by deployment policy. Transport APIs, physical
connector locality, bus-level anti-relay guarantees, trust-anchor enrollment,
and endorsement authoring are outside scope.

Workload attestation, tenant attestation, confidential-computing device
assignment, and Trusted Device Interface Security Protocol (TDISP) device
attachment are outside the scope of this profile.

# Conventions and Terminology

The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "REQUIRED", "SHALL", "SHALL NOT", "SHOULD",
"SHOULD NOT", "RECOMMENDED", "NOT RECOMMENDED", "MAY", and "OPTIONAL" in this
document are to be interpreted as described in BCP 14 [@{ietf-rfc2119}]
[@{ietf-rfc8174}] when, and only when, they appear in all capitals.

Data structures are defined using the Concise Data Definition Language (CDDL)
[@{ietf-rfc8610}].

This document uses Attester, Verifier, Relying Party, Endorser, Reference Value
Provider, Lead Attester, Sub-Attester, Composite Device, Target Environment, and
Attesting Environment as defined in [@{ietf-rfc9334}].

Detached Claims-Set:
: The native Sub-Attester evidence for one Target Environment, encoded as a CBOR
  Claims-Set and conveyed in the detached slot of the Detached EAT Bundle. A
  digest in the signed Composite EAT commits to it.

Target Environment Identifier:
: A stable topology label rendered as an `env.*` string and used as the map key
  for a Sub-Attester in both the signed `submods` claim and detached Claims-Set
  map.

# Architecture and Trust Model

## Composite Device Architecture and Trust Boundaries

This profile applies the RFC 9334 composite-device pattern. The Lead Attester
constructs and signs the main token, including the nonce, its identity and
measurements, the reported Target Environment Identifiers, and the detached
Claims-Set digests. The Lead Attester does not appear in `submods`; its claims
are top-level claims.

Sub-Attesters produce independently protected native Evidence. The Lead
Attester signature does not make the Lead Attester authoritative for that
Evidence. The Verifier validates the Composite EAT signature and each
Sub-Attester's native protection, then appraises the reported composition using
independently trusted expected-composition information supplied by an Endorser,
Reference Value Provider, or other source authorized by Verifier policy.

In a BMC-mediated implementation, the Lead Attester can be a Root of Trust
associated with the BMC. The BMC RoT protects the Composite EAT signing key,
supplies the Lead Attester identity and local measurements, and produces the
signed main token.

## Delegated Collection and Bundle-Assembly Functions

Evidence collection and bundle assembly are implementation functions that MAY
be delegated to one or more supporting components, either within or outside the
Lead Attester's protected boundary. These functions may discover Sub-Attesters,
obtain native Evidence, construct detached Claims-Sets, and assemble or convey
the final bundle. In a BMC-mediated implementation, BMC software commonly
performs these functions outside the BMC RoT's protected signing boundary.

Delegation does not transfer Lead Attester authority. Supporting components may
supply the nonce, reported Target Environment Identifiers, and detached
Claims-Set digest records as inputs, and may combine the signed main token with
the detached Claims-Sets. They cannot produce the Lead Attester signature and
are not authoritative for native Evidence authenticity, collection
completeness, or Target Environment Identifier correctness.

A collection implementation SHOULD use an authenticated collection policy, or
an equivalent trusted mechanism, to mitigate the risk of accidental or
malicious omission during Evidence collection. Such hardening improves
collection coverage and integrity. It does not establish platform-inventory
completeness or make the collection implementation authoritative for expected
platform composition.

## Threat Model

| Threat | Mitigation or detection |
| --- | --- |
| Main-token forgery | Validate the COSE_Sign1 signature and Lead Attester certificate path. |
| Lead Attester certificate misbinding | Match protected `x5t` to the end-entity certificate in `x5chain`. |
| Composite EAT replay | Match `eat_nonce` to the Verifier challenge. |
| Detached Claims-Set modification | Recompute the digest committed to by the signed `submods` entry. |
| Fabricated native Evidence | Validate the Sub-Attester's native signature or other protection. |
| Native Evidence replay | Verify that the native Evidence binds the same nonce. |
| Omission from one bundle map | Require identical `submods` and detached Claims-Set key sets. |
| Omission from both maps | Compare the reported environment set with independently trusted expected-composition information. |
| Target Environment Identifier mislabeling | Compare the proven native identity with the identity authorized for that environment. |
| Expected-composition substitution | Authenticate and authorize the expected-composition source according to Verifier policy. |
| Lead Attester compromise | Treat the platform-scoped binding as compromised; independently protected Sub-Attester Evidence still requires separate validation. |

## Security Properties and Limits

The Composite EAT signature establishes integrity and freshness of the included
platform observation. It does not independently establish platform-inventory
completeness, Target Environment Identifier correctness, physical locality, or
protection against bus-level relay. Those properties require Verifier appraisal
against independently trusted expected-composition information and, where
applicable, additional transport or hardware assurances.

# Composite EAT Profile

## Profile Identifier

This specification requests assignment of the following object identifier (OID)
from the OCP Security branch:

`ocp-composite-eat OBJECT IDENTIFIER ::= {1 3 6 1 4 1 42623 1 4}`

The requested dotted-decimal value is `1.3.6.1.4.1.42623.1.4`. This value is a
proposed assignment pending confirmation by OCP and is not assigned or reserved
by publication of this working draft. Implementations MUST NOT emit it as an
assigned profile identifier until OCP approves the assignment.

After approval, the `eat_profile` claim (claim 265) MUST contain this OID. In
CBOR, it MUST use the preferred PEN-relative encoding with tag 112 defined by
[@{ietf-rfc9090}]. The resulting diagnostic notation is
`112(h'82cc7f0104')`.

The OID `1.3.6.1.4.1.42623.1.3` identifies the OCP Profile for IETF Entity
Attestation Token and MUST NOT be used as the profile identifier of the
Composite EAT main token. A device-emitted EAT carried as Sub-Attester Evidence
MUST conform to the OCP Profile for IETF Entity Attestation Token and identify
that profile in its own `eat_profile` claim. This requirement does not require a
Sub-Attester that supplies native SPDM Evidence to emit an EAT.

## Profile Conformance {#profile-conformance}

This section resolves the profile items in Section 6 of [@{ietf-rfc9711}].

| Profile item | This profile |
| ------------ | ------------ |
| Profile identifier | Requested OID `1.3.6.1.4.1.42623.1.4`; pending OCP approval |
| Serialization | CBOR satisfying the core deterministic encoding requirements of Section 4.2.1 of [@{ietf-rfc8949}] |
| Top-level message | Detached EAT Bundle with tag 602 [@{ietf-rfc9711}] |
| Top-level media type | `application/eat-bun+cbor` [@{ietf-rfc9782}] |
| Main token | CWT tag 61 containing COSE_Sign1 tag 18 |
| Token protection | Exactly one COSE_Sign1 [@{ietf-rfc9052}] using ES384, algorithm -35 [@{ietf-rfc9053}]; MAC and encryption structures are prohibited |
| Detached digest | SHA-384, algorithm -43, with a 48-byte output [@{ietf-rfc9053}] |
| Key identification | Protected SHA-384 `x5t`, label 34, and unprotected leaf-first `x5chain`, label 33 [@{ietf-rfc9360}] |
| Endorsement identification | None; `x5chain` identifies the verification key and is not an endorsement identifier |
| Freshness | Exactly one 32-byte `eat_nonce`, unique for each request |
| Main token content type | `application/eat+cwt` [@{ietf-rfc9782}] |
| Lead measurements | TCG DICE Concise Evidence using CoAP Content-Format 10571 (`application/ce+cbor`) |
| Detached evidence claim | CWT `cmw` claim 299 [@{ietf-rfc9999}] |
| Additional claims | Prohibited by the closed CDDL maps in this profile |
{: title="Composite EAT profile conformance"}

Conforming producers MUST emit every choice in the table. Conforming Verifiers
MUST support those choices. A producer MUST NOT add claims to
`composite-eat-claims` or `detached-claims-set` beyond those permitted by the
closed CDDL maps. A Verifier MUST reject a token or detached Claims-Set
containing an additional claim.

This is a full EAT profile in the sense of Section 6.2 of [@{ietf-rfc9711}]. The
classification applies to decoding, verifying, and checking freshness of the
Composite EAT envelope. Native Evidence in claim 299 is claim content whose
appraisal is governed by its identified format and Verifier policy. A Verifier
MUST NOT produce a successful Attestation Result for a native Evidence item
unless it can validate that item locally or delegate validation to a trusted
Verifier that supports the identified format and algorithms.

## Bundle Structure {#bundle}

The authoritative artifact is a Detached EAT Bundle with CBOR tag 602. Slot 1
is the signed Composite EAT (`main-token`). Slot 2 is the detached Claims-Set
map keyed by Target Environment Identifier. When conveyed with a media type,
the bundle MUST use `application/eat-bun+cbor`.

~~~ cddl
composite-bundle = #6.602([
  main-token: bstr .cbor composite-eat,
  detached-claims-sets: {
    + env-id => bstr .cbor detached-claims-set
  }
])

composite-eat = #6.61(#6.18([
  protected:   bstr .cbor protected-headers,
  unprotected: unprotected-headers,
  payload:     bstr .cbor composite-eat-claims,
  signature:   bstr
]))

protected-headers = {
  1:  -35,
  3:  "application/eat+cwt",
  34: certificate-thumbprint
}

certificate-thumbprint = [ -43, bstr .size 48 ]

unprotected-headers = {
  33: cose-x509-chain
}

cose-x509-chain = bstr / [ 2* bstr ]
~~~

Every certificate in `cose-x509-chain` is DER encoded. A chain containing more
than one certificate MUST begin with the Lead Attester end-entity certificate.
The protected `x5t` MUST use SHA-384 and equal the SHA-384 digest of the complete
DER encoding of that certificate.

A Verifier MUST reject the token if `x5t` does not match the first certificate
in `x5chain`. It MUST treat every carried certificate as untrusted input and
validate a path to a trust anchor configured independently of the bundle,
including applicable certificate usage, validity, and revocation checks
[@{ietf-rfc5280}].

## Top-Level Claims {#top-claims}

~~~ cddl
composite-eat-claims = {
  10:  bstr .size 32,
  265: profile-oid,
  256: bstr .size (7..33),
  273: measurements-type,
  266: submods-type
}
~~~

The `eat_nonce` claim contains exactly one nonce. The Verifier MUST generate a
new nonce for every request. The same nonce MUST be used to obtain every
nonce-capable Sub-Attester evidence item included in the bundle.

Claim 265 MUST equal the OCP Composite EAT profile OID approved before final
publication. The requested value is not an assigned profile identifier until
OCP approves it.

## Lead Attester Self-Measurements {#self-meas}

The Lead Attester's local evidence is carried in top-level claim 273, not in
`submods`.

~~~ cddl
measurements-type = [ + lead-attester-concise-evidence ]

lead-attester-concise-evidence = [
  content-format: 10571,
  value: bstr .cbor concise-evidence-map
]
~~~

This constrains the `measurements` claim from [@{ietf-rfc9711}]. Each entry MUST
use CoAP Content-Format 10571 (`application/ce+cbor`) and contain a
byte-string-wrapped `concise-evidence-map` conforming to
[@{tcg-dice-concise-evidence-binding-for-spdm}]. This is the same measurement
encoding required by the OCP Profile for IETF Entity Attestation Token. The
Concise Evidence carried here describes Target Environments measured by the
Lead Attester; Sub-Attester Evidence remains detached under `submods`.

# Detached Sub-Attester Evidence {#detached-evidence}

## Detached-Submodule-Digest Binding {#digest-binding}

Every Sub-Attester is represented in the signed token by a
Detached-Submodule-Digest. The complete evidence is carried in slot 2 of the
bundle under the same Target Environment Identifier.

~~~ cddl
submods-type = { + env-id => detached-submodule-digest }

detached-submodule-digest = [
  hash-alg: -43,
  digest:   bstr .size 48
]

env-id = tstr .size (5..64)
~~~

For every Target Environment Identifier E, `submods[E]` MUST equal the SHA-384
digest of `detached-claims-sets[E]` computed according to
[Deterministic Encoding and Digest Rules](#digest-rules). The key set of
`submods` MUST be identical to the key set of `detached-claims-sets`. A Verifier
MUST reject a bundle containing a missing or unreferenced detached Claims-Set.

## Deterministic Encoding and Digest Rules {#digest-rules}

Each detached Claims-Set MUST satisfy the core deterministic encoding
requirements of Section 4.2.1 of [@{ietf-rfc8949}]. A Verifier MUST reject a
Claims-Set that does not satisfy those requirements.

The digest input is the encoded Claims-Set bytes directly, not the byte-string
wrapper used by the bundle and not a textual encoding. SHA-384, COSE algorithm
-43, MUST be used for every detached-submodule digest. This algorithm is
independent of a measurement hash negotiated by a native protocol.

## Native Evidence Carriage {#native-evidence}

Each detached Claims-Set contains registered CWT claim 299 (`cmw`) from
[@{ietf-rfc9999}]. Its value is one CMW Record. The CMW type identifies the
native evidence format.

~~~ cddl
detached-claims-set = {
  299 => native-evidence-cmw
}

native-evidence-cmw = spdm-evidence-cmw /
                      device-eat-cmw /
                      composite-eat-bundle-cmw
~~~

Each CMW carries the Evidence conceptual-message indicator. Evidence is bit 2
in the RFC 9999 `ind` bitmap and is encoded as integer 4. CMW is encapsulation,
not security protection. A Verifier MUST validate native protection and
provenance after validating the detached digest.

### SPDM Signed Measurements

~~~ cddl
spdm-evidence-cmw = [
  type: "application/spdm-evidence+cbor",
  value: bstr .cbor spdm-evidence,
  ind: 4
]

spdm-evidence = [
  signed-measurements: bstr,
  cert-chain: bstr,
  ? vca: bstr
]
~~~

`signed-measurements` is the binary SPDM SignedMeasurements value. For SPDM 1.2
and later, it includes the negotiated-version, capabilities, and algorithms
(VCA) transcript. For SPDM 1.0 and 1.1, it contains only the signed
`GET_MEASUREMENTS` exchange.

`cert-chain` is the concatenation, in SPDM chain order, of the DER-encoded
certificates extracted from the reassembled SPDM certificate chain. It excludes
the SPDM `Length`, `Reserved`, and `RootHash` fields and is not PEM encoded.

`vca` is the observed VCA transcript. It MUST be present for SPDM 1.0 and 1.1
and MUST be absent for SPDM 1.2 and later. See [@{dmtf-dsp0274}].

### Device-Emitted EAT

~~~ cddl
device-eat-cmw = [
  type: "application/eat+cwt",
  device-token: bstr,
  ind: 4
]
~~~

`device-token` MUST contain the complete CWT-encoded EAT as emitted by the
device, without decoding or re-encoding. It MUST cryptographically bind the
Verifier nonce. It MUST conform to the OCP Profile for IETF Entity Attestation
Token and MUST identify that profile in claim 265. Other registered EAT media
types require a revision of this profile.

### Recursive Composite EAT

A Sub-Attester that is itself a Lead Attester MAY supply a complete Composite
EAT bundle as native evidence.

~~~ cddl
composite-eat-bundle-cmw = [
  type: "application/eat-bun+cbor",
  value: bstr .cbor composite-bundle,
  ind: 4
]
~~~

The child bundle MUST conform to this profile and MUST bind the same Verifier nonce
as its parent. The Verifier MUST recursively perform the validation in
[Verification](#verification). Implementations SHOULD enforce a
deployment-specific maximum nesting depth.

### SPDM-Carried TCG DICE Concise Evidence

Signed measurements carrying TCG DICE Concise Evidence
[@{tcg-dice-concise-evidence-binding-for-spdm}] use the
`spdm-evidence-cmw` schema. The measurement blocks are preserved verbatim. The
component performing collection does not need to distinguish ordinary SPDM
measurements from blocks carrying TCG DICE Concise Evidence. The Verifier
interprets those blocks under the applicable native Evidence profile.

### CMW and Digest Boundaries

The CMW is part of the detached Claims-Set. The digest input therefore includes
claim key 299, the complete deterministic CMW encoding, its `type` and `ind`
fields, and its value byte string.

A conveying protocol MAY additionally carry the complete `composite-bundle` as
a CMW typed value. Such an outer wrapper is not part of a detached Claims-Set
digest input.

## Target Environment Identifier {#env-id}

The `submods` key and detached Claims-Set map key is a stable Target Environment
Identifier, distinct from transport addresses, device UUIDs, and endorsement
identifiers. It:

1. is lowercase ASCII;
2. has at least two dot-separated components, with first component `env`;
3. names a reported logical environment, not the device instance; and
4. is between 5 and 64 bytes long.

Examples include `env.nic.0`, `env.gpu.0`, and `env.pcie.4`.

# Verification (Informative) {#verification}

A Verifier is expected to perform the following checks before producing an
Attestation Result:

1. Decode the bundle, confirm tag 602, and enforce every encoding and algorithm
   choice in [Profile Conformance](#profile-conformance).
2. Match protected `x5t` to the end-entity certificate in `x5chain`, validate
   the Lead Attester certificate path, and validate the COSE_Sign1 signature.
3. Match `eat_nonce` to the challenge, match `eat_profile` to the approved OCP
  Composite EAT profile identifier, and validate the Lead Attester `ueid` and
  measurements.
4. Require identical `submods` and detached Claims-Set key sets and recompute
   every digest according to [Deterministic Encoding and Digest Rules](#digest-rules).
5. Parse each claim-299 CMW, validate its native signature and certificate or
   token chain, and match its nonce to the challenge.
6. Match the identity proven by each native Evidence item to the identity
  authorized for its reported Target Environment Identifier by independently
  trusted composition information.

The Composite EAT signature, matching key sets, and detached digests establish
the integrity and freshness of the included platform observation. Digest
validation detects replacement of an included detached Claims-Set, and native
Evidence validation establishes its Sub-Attester provenance. Determining
whether authentic Evidence is associated with the correct logical environment
requires independent appraisal.

A Verifier MUST NOT infer platform-inventory completeness solely from the
Composite EAT signature or from agreement between the signed `submods` map and
the detached Claims-Set map. A Verifier that determines platform-inventory
completeness MUST compare the reported environment set and proven native
identities against independently trusted expected-composition information. A
platform CoRIM [@{ietf-rats-corim}] is one possible source. Native measurements
are appraised against trusted reference values selected by Verifier policy.
This profile does not define endorsement discovery or reference-value
appraisal.

# Security Considerations {#security}

The security considerations of [@{ietf-rfc8392}], [@{ietf-rfc9052}],
[@{ietf-rfc9360}], [@{ietf-rfc9711}], [@{ietf-rfc9999}], and
[@{ietf-rfc9334}] apply.

## Native Evidence Protection

Native signatures remain end-to-end verifiable because evidence is carried
verbatim and pinned by a signed digest. A Verifier MUST validate both layers.
Accepting the Composite EAT signature without validating native evidence would
incorrectly treat collection-component-authored bytes as
Sub-Attester-authored Evidence.

Each native Evidence item MUST independently prove binding to the same nonce.
A Verifier MUST reject an item whose nonce is absent, unverifiable, or does not
match the challenge.

The fixed ES384 and SHA-384 choices prevent algorithm substitution at the
Composite EAT layer. For SPDM 1.0 and 1.1, the separately carried VCA transcript
is not covered by the responder's measurement signature. A deployment requiring
cryptographic binding of negotiated SPDM parameters MUST require SPDM 1.2 or
later in appraisal policy.

## Lead Attester Protection

The Lead Attester signing key and sources of `ueid` and top-level measurements
MUST be protected from untrusted software performing Evidence collection or
bundle assembly. The `ueid` MUST follow [@{ietf-rfc9711}] and remain stable for
the Lead Attester.

Compromise of the Lead Attester signing key permits forgery of the envelope,
nonce, Lead Attester measurements, labels, and evidence digests. It does not
permit forgery of an uncompromised Sub-Attester signature but defeats the
platform-scoped binding. Verifiers MUST validate the Lead Attester certificate
path, revocation state, and authorization policy rather than trusting a chain
because it is carried in `x5chain`.

Verifiers MUST perform the certificate-path and protected `x5t` validation
specified in [Bundle Structure](#bundle).

## Resource Exhaustion

Detached evidence, certificate chains, recursive bundles, and the number of
submodules are attacker-influenced inputs. Implementations SHOULD enforce
limits on bundle size, Claims-Set size, certificate-chain length, submodule
count, and nesting depth. Verifiers SHOULD validate structure and signed
digests before expensive native appraisal, without treating a valid digest as
a substitute for native signature validation.

# Privacy Considerations

A stable Lead Attester `ueid` and end-entity certificate enable correlation.
Native device certificates and device-emitted EATs can identify individual
Sub-Attesters. Target Environment Identifiers expose topology, and measurements
can reveal firmware versions or configuration.

Deployments SHOULD restrict bundle access to authorized Verifiers and use a
confidentiality-protected conveying protocol when this information is
sensitive. A Verifier SHOULD disclose only the Attestation Result needed by a
Relying Party rather than forwarding the bundle. Nonces SHOULD be unpredictable
and MUST NOT encode user, account, workload, or other identifying information.

# IANA Considerations

This OCP specification requests no IANA action. It uses
`application/spdm-evidence+cbor`, whose registration is requested by the aligned
IETF Composite EAT specification [@{draft-sun-rats-composite-eat}]. The media
type identifies the deterministic CBOR encoding of `spdm-evidence` defined in
this document. Until the registration is complete, implementations remain
subject to the status of that IETF request.

# Alignment and Change Control

This profile adopts the Composite EAT mechanism defined in
`draft-sun-rats-composite-eat-00` [@{draft-sun-rats-composite-eat}] and applies
additional constraints for OCP platform inventory attestation. Unless
constrained by this specification, the Composite EAT structure and processing
rules remain aligned with the IETF specification.

The IETF and OCP specifications define separate full EAT profiles. A token
identifies exactly one of them through claim 265; it does not identify both.
Conformance to this OCP profile is determined by this specification. In
particular, when an OCP Composite EAT carries a device-emitted EAT as
Sub-Attester Evidence, that EAT MUST conform to the OCP Profile for IETF Entity
Attestation Token. Native SPDM Evidence remains permitted and need not be
converted to a device EAT. This OCP profile also constrains the Lead Attester's
claim 273 measurements to the TCG DICE Concise Evidence encoding used by the
OCP Profile for IETF Entity Attestation Token.

This specification requests OID `1.3.6.1.4.1.42623.1.4` for the OCP Composite
EAT profile. The request follows the existing OCP Security branch and uses the
next child value after the documented `.2` and `.3` assignments. OCP remains the
assignment authority and MUST confirm that the requested child value is
available before final publication. The OCP Profile for IETF Entity Attestation
Token OID, `1.3.6.1.4.1.42623.1.3`, identifies a different full EAT profile and
cannot be reused for the Composite EAT main token. After OCP approval, the
profile CDDL MUST be narrowed to the exact assigned OID encoding. Final
publication also depends on registration of `application/spdm-evidence+cbor`
through the aligned IETF work.

Changes to bundle structure, claims, algorithms, native evidence forms,
deterministic encoding, digest input, or verification behavior require explicit
OCP review. An incompatible change requires a new OCP profile identifier. When
the IETF work becomes an RFC, this document SHOULD replace the draft reference
with the RFC and document whether any technical differences remain.

